## 0.6.37 — Reach-gating correction (Tier vs Cost)

The 0.6.36 work above was wrong, and the live verification at the end of that entry actively
reinforced the mistake instead of catching it: it read `system.tier` as the field gated by Reach
("Availability"), based on a plausible-sounding but incorrect cross-reference — Part II's
character-creation text repeatedly says "availability... must not exceed your Reach," and Part
VIII never uses the word "Availability," so the previous session concluded Part VIII's "Tier" must
be the same concept Part II calls "Availability." That's wrong. The project owner supplied an
authoritative reference document (saved verbatim at `design/reach-and-economy.md`) making the
actual model explicit:

- Reach is a character's **tier of economic access**, not a currency or spend value.
- The Equipment table's `system.cost` field (a pre-existing StringField, already documented in
  `bulk-import.mjs`'s own CSV import docs as "Reach cost to acquire/use" — a fact this project had
  already established once and then failed to recall when doing the 0.6.36 work) **is** the Reach
  gate. Its UI label was "Cost," which is also wrong — it isn't a spend value — and should read
  "Reach."
- `system.tier` is a completely different, legitimate field: the sophistication rating used by the
  Chassis/Fitting/Component modular equipment system (see
  `design/chassis-fitting-augment-system.md`). It has nothing to do with Reach. The Components &
  Augments table's use of `c.tier` (character-sheet.hbs, npc-sheet.hbs, actor-sheet.mjs,
  npc-sheet.mjs) was already correct and untouched by this fix.

**Fix**: added `computeReachGate(equipmentSystem, effectiveReach)` to `module/utils.mjs` — parses
`system.cost` as a number (empty string means "no stated Reach requirement," not 0, unlike
`parseSigned`'s 0-fallback used for Fortitude/Resilience/Movement) and applies any
`reachExceptionSource`/`reachExceptionMargin`. Switched the over-Reach warning logic in
`actor-sheet.mjs`, `npc-sheet.mjs`, and `character-wizard.mjs` to call it instead of reading
`item.system.tier`. Reverted every "Tier (Availability)" label back to plain "Tier"
(`equipment-sheet.hbs`) and relabeled the `system.cost` field/column "Reach" everywhere it's shown
or edited (`equipment-sheet.hbs`, `character-sheet.hbs`, `npc-sheet.hbs`, `wizard.hbs`).

**Lesson for future sessions**: when a field's purpose is ambiguous between two source documents
that use different terminology, check whether the codebase already has an existing field
documented for exactly that purpose (in this case `system.cost`, documented in `bulk-import.mjs`)
before introducing new relabeling logic elsewhere — and don't let a live test that merely confirms
"the warning icon renders when the number is high" stand in for confirming it's reading the
*correct* number. The test procedure looked convincing (set Tier to 3, saw a warning) precisely
because both fields are plain numbers on the same item type, so a test against the wrong field
still "worked."

